To make Bootstrap tables sortable alphanumerically without using jQuery or other plugins, you can use the HTML5 Drag and Drop API. Here’s a step-by-step guide:

1. **HTML Structure**: Ensure your table has a structure that supports sorting. Each header cell (`<th>`) should have a `data-sort` attribute to indicate the column it sorts.

2. **CSS Styling**: Add some CSS to style the table and the headers to indicate they are sortable. For example, you can add a cursor pointer to the headers to show they are clickable.

3. **JavaScript Implementation**: Use the HTML5 Drag and Drop API to handle the sorting logic. Here’s a basic example:

```html
<table class="table" id="sortableTable">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th data-sort="name">Name</th>
      <th data-sort="age">Age</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr data-name="Alice" data-age="30">
      <td>Alice</td>
      <td>30</td>
    </tr>
    <tr data-name="Bob" data-age="25">
      <td>Bob</td>
      <td>25</td>
    </tr>
    <tr data-name="Charlie" data-age="35">
      <td>Charlie</td>
      <td>35</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<script>
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
  const table = document.getElementById('sortableTable');
  const headers = table.querySelectorAll('th[data-sort]');
  const rows = table.querySelectorAll('tbody tr');

  headers.forEach(header => {
    header.addEventListener('click', function() {
      const sortKey = this.getAttribute('data-sort');
      const isAscending = !this.classList.contains('descending');

      rows.sort((a, b) => {
        const valA = a.getAttribute(`data-${sortKey}`);
        const valB = b.getAttribute(`data-${sortKey}`);

        if (valA < valB) return isAscending ? -1 : 1;
        if (valA > valB) return isAscending ? 1 : -1;
        return 0;
      });

      Array.from(rows).forEach(row => table.querySelector('tbody').appendChild(row));

      this.classList.toggle('descending');
    });
  });
});
</script>
```

This code sets up a basic sortable table where clicking on a header sorts the rows based on the corresponding data attribute. The `data-sort` attribute specifies the column to sort by, and the `data-name` and `data-age` attributes store the actual data for sorting.

For a more robust solution, you can explore the [SortableJS](https://github.com/SortableJS/Sortable) library, which provides a more comprehensive set of features and better performance. However, it does require including the library in your project.

